{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2015 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7f19a51db5706a4b8378a9ab35d3dca546627606387689bcc0b518603b8fdedf" :edb/db-before-t 2014 :edb/db-after-t 2015 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535327 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:08:33.168Z" 13194139535327 true] [17592186179642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343916 13194139535327 true] [17592186179642 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343923 13194139535327 true] [17592186179649 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343902 13194139535327 true] [17592186179649 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343909 13194139535327 true] [17592186294517 1145 "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)" 13194139535327 false] [17592186294517 1145 "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=-1,$ which gives the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=0,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-y=2,$ that is, the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/77a54ef31c41e0246e3d337a69c7faa161194058e6a5c672ef24d86866a83faf.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move downward and away from the origin $O.$ \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/4e124d5e77ef9a28002aba9aac40f0b7eff1232dd1c08b6bca10b166469675c7.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/8bd5b7575d1693935df0d25a259767f0dd267fc1153139cd55cebad75e3988fd.png)" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343902 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343903 13194139535327 true] [17592186343902 1089 "q-122693" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343902 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535327 true] [17592186343902 1100 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}?$" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343902 1113 #uuid "d89542da-1193-5d73-b783-8e2dd0df7f2d" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343902 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i+y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,1) & =-\\,i+j \\\\ ∇f(2,2) & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i+(-1)j=2\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png)" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343904 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343905 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343906 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343907 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343908 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1041 "selection" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343905 13194139535327 true] [17592186343903 1110 #uuid "2e60660e-7547-5b05-9688-5fcb7923b18b" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343904 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/0ea540eb4a48d1fb1777e2d6397e6d38937e9795c4d810b48c0fda0801d1c9ba.png" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535327 true] [17592186343904 1111 #uuid "eb43086b-f35c-5d10-be54-1915fe10d961" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343905 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/369ebf19405768bcca19f4981f0237647836250548b0a33f9b4fdba92d85ddc9.png" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535327 true] [17592186343905 1111 #uuid "8d432a70-8dd7-5c93-9e5b-0b57dc7f77be" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343906 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/f4f0b2e96fa7ee0f1dca7d57a2815e93d77665c0285fa81f2a8b2c5965a39415.png" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343906 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535327 true] [17592186343906 1111 #uuid "828d0603-44f5-577f-93da-a0a969396712" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343907 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/d3fc823cfcea400219e553f9e6911a312f0a14c7f39aafb49d1af3463cb3e46b.png" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535327 true] [17592186343907 1111 #uuid "b7074bdf-1a20-5dc6-b4f3-4d7bcdc8cf00" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343908 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/ba42a974a78df2c0d228e8f5ae543019841c8a7b47e38f02e139fe2d97b8ecf5.png" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535327 true] [17592186343908 1111 #uuid "25820380-a156-5def-ab99-a66f13621cd8" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343909 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343910 13194139535327 true] [17592186343909 1089 "q-122732" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535327 true] [17592186343909 1100 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}?$" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343909 1113 #uuid "14b7ceda-a408-53c9-aeb5-05da06c84fba" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343909 1145 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(2,1),$ and $(-2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1\\,j=i-4j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & ={1}^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\,j=i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\,i+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,j=i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/020c3ec7cd0e5e57bb4c6de8516988187c9038d9e5598b116966b71fa8b917d8.png)" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343911 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343912 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343913 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343914 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343915 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1041 "selection" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343912 13194139535327 true] [17592186343910 1110 #uuid "7da31b53-f741-518d-8db1-e178ff46ee68" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343911 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062668/assets/0c425b6fcb88f4c958a7c0371a64a815eda5ebf8ed14b0adc06875de1e24003b.png" 13194139535327 true] [17592186343911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535327 true] [17592186343911 